this bad weather isn't stopping Lucy  from carrying out his work today he's on  a special mission in the center of  Shanghai we're speaking out against  ignorance and discrimination against  people who are hiv-positive Lucy and the  other activists walk through the city in  small groups it's a way of spreading  their message that avoids drawing the  attention of the authorities always  sensitive when it comes to  demonstrations the group go to several  locations across the city it's a  strategy that's not without risks their  presence on the streets might escape  official scrutiny but the photos they  post online could also attract attention  lose she works for the organization  Beijing gender he writes information  brochures about living with HIV and  gives talks in schools Lou is 24 years  old and was diagnosed with HIV for years  ago fighter league when I found out  about my infection I stayed completely  calm I simply decided that I didn't want  to live anymore and I planned all of the  details of my suicide but a friend held  him back from carrying out his plan and  after that Lou decided to share his  experience educating others about living  with HIV has become his vocation hong  kong's war there are difficulties  everywhere at work in life those who are  infected lose their jobs and are evicted  from their apartments some doctors even  refused to treat them there's  discrimination in many areas of life she  should I feel good  it's problems like these that the  Chinese HIV activists want to bring to  the attention of people in Shanghai it's  desperately needed both Chinese society  and the Chinese government have a little  understanding of how to deal with people  who are hiv-positive that makes the work  of people like new shoe all the more  important but means they tread a narrow  line between bringing attention to their  cause and drawing unwanted attention  from the authorities
 

